<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Mining yield (system: mining; RimWorld: StatDefOf.MiningYield). Its own file rather than another entry in
    Stats_Work.xml, which three other lanes have been editing (CLAUDE.md: add a file rather than edit a
    shared one). Read by Things/MineableUtility.YieldFor for any vein whose def says its yield is wasteable;
    bound through Stats/MiningStatDefOf.

    RimWorld does scale mined yield by the miner's skill — a poor miner wastes part of the vein — and this
    port can express that through the same skillNeedFactors pipeline Stats_Work.xml already uses for
    MiningSpeed. The CURVE, though, is not RimWorld's: as that file's own header says, RimWorld's real
    StatDefs_Work.xml is not available to check against from here. So this is a SkillNeed_BaseBonus chosen to
    be monotonic and to land somewhere plausible — a beginner recovers well under half a vein, a level-20
    miner recovers all of it and no more, because maxValue caps it there. A miner cannot conjure ore that was
    never in the rock; the skill decides only how much of it survives being dug out.

    The tests assert the trend (a better miner recovers more of the same vein, level 20 recovers all of it),
    never the numbers below.
  -->

  <StatDef>
    <defName>MiningYield</defName>
    <label>mining yield</label>
    <description>How much of a mined vein this pawn actually recovers, rather than wasting in the rubble.</description>
    <defaultBaseValue>1</defaultBaseValue>
    <minValue>0.05</minValue>
    <maxValue>1</maxValue>
    <skillNeedFactors>
      <li Class="SimWorld.Stats.SkillNeed_BaseBonus">
        <skill>Mining</skill>
        <baseValue>0.4</baseValue>
        <bonusPerLevel>0.03</bonusPerLevel>
      </li>
    </skillNeedFactors>
  </StatDef>

</Defs>
